# Array Operations – Time and Space Complexity

## Overview

Arrays store elements in contiguous memory locations.
Because of this, accessing elements is fast,
but insertion and deletion can be costly.

--------------------------------------------------

## Complexity Table

| Operation                         | Time Complexity (Worst Case) | Space Complexity | Explanation |
|----------------------------------|-------------------------------|------------------|------------|
| Access by Index (arr[i])        | O(1)                          | O(1)             | Direct address calculation using index. |
| Update by Index                  | O(1)                          | O(1)             | Direct replacement at given index. |
| Traversal                        | O(n)                          | O(1)             | Visit each element once. |
| Linear Search                    | O(n)                          | O(1)             | May need to check all elements. |
| Binary Search (sorted array)     | O(log n)                      | O(1)             | Array divided in half each step. |
| Insertion at Beginning           | O(n)                          | O(1)             | All elements must shift right. |
| Insertion at Middle              | O(n)                          | O(1)             | Elements after position must shift. |
| Insertion at End                 | O(1)                          | O(1)             | Direct insert if space available. |
| Deletion at Beginning            | O(n)                          | O(1)             | All elements must shift left. |
| Deletion at Middle               | O(n)                          | O(1)             | Elements after position must shift. |
| Deletion at End                  | O(1)                          | O(1)             | Just reduce size. |
| Finding Maximum / Minimum        | O(n)                          | O(1)             | Must traverse entire array. |
| Sorting (Bubble / Selection)     | O(n²)                         | O(1)             | Nested loops. |
| Sorting (Merge Sort)             | O(n log n)                    | O(n)             | Divide and merge using extra space. |
| Sorting (Quick Sort)             | O(n²) worst, O(n log n) avg   | O(log n)         | Recursive stack space used. |

--------------------------------------------------

## Important Notes

1. Arrays provide constant-time access because:
   Address = Base Address + (index × size of element)

2. Insertion and deletion are costly because:
   Elements must shift to maintain order in contiguous memory.

3. Binary Search works only when the array is sorted.

4. Worst case complexity is usually reported in interviews.

--------------------------------------------------

## Summary

Arrays are:
- Fast for accessing elements (O(1))
- Slow for insertion/deletion in middle (O(n))
- Efficient for searching only when sorted (Binary Search: O(log n))
